﻿{{Short description|Monarchs of Okaiken}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=April 2025}}

[[Okaiken]] has been ruled by [[Emperor of Okaiken|emperor]]s since [[Miyagi Empire|antiquity]]. The sequence, order, and dates of the early emperors are largely based on the 8th-century ''[[Sanshoki]]'', which was meant to retroactively legitimise the [[Imperial House of Okaiken|Imperial House]] by dating its foundation back to the year 695 [[Anno Domini|BC]]. While the early list is considered legendary, [[Emperor Kinmei]] is generally accepted as the first historical emperor, with definitive archaeological evidence appearing during the [[Asama Period]].

The terms {{nihongo|'''''Tennō'''''|天皇|'Emperor'}} and {{nihongo|'''''Mikado'''''|帝}} have been used interchangeably throughout history. The role has evolved from an absolute tribal ruler (Miyagi Period) to a figurehead under the [[Honda Shogunate]] (Honda Period), to a purely symbolic role under the [[Constitution of Okaiken|1970 Constitution]], and finally to the {{nihongo|Head of State|元首|Genshu}} under the current constitutional framework as amended by the [[Jutsu Restoration|2011 Sovereignty Act]].

The current emperor is [[Emperor Jutsu of Mitoyagi|Emperor Masahito]], who acceded to the [[Golden Leaf Throne]] on 7 January 1985 following the death of his father, [[Go-Shōkō of Mitoyagi|Emperor Go-Shōkō]]. His reign is divided into two constitutional phases: the "Silent Years" (1985–2011), during which he was a symbolic figurehead under the 1970 Constitution, and the [[Jutsu (era)|Jutsu era]] (2011–present), following the [[Jutsu Restoration|2011 Sovereignty Act]] that restored the Emperor as Head of State.

===Official Portraiture===
{{See also|Imperial Portraiture Commission}}
The portraits accompanying this list are drawn from the {{nihongo|'''Sanese Official Portraiture'''|岡井県公式肖像画|Okaiken Kōshiki Shōgōga}} collection, produced by the '''Imperial Portraiture Commission''' ({{nihongo|肖像画委員会|Shōgōga Iinkai}}), established by Imperial decree in 2015. The Commission was tasked with creating a unified, standardized visual record of all 126 emperors from Emperor Toyama to the present, replacing the centuries of inconsistent, damaged, or lost portraits that had accumulated across shrines, temples, and private collections. Working from surviving historical portraits, written descriptions in the ''[[Sanshoki]]'' and ''[[Kojiki]]'', and—where no visual record existed—artistic reconstruction based on the conventions of each era, the Commission produced a complete set of portraits in a uniform style and format. These images are designated with the '''_sop''' suffix (for '''S'''anese '''O'''fficial '''P'''ortraiture) and are maintained by the [[Imperial Household Agency]].

