==Notes on the constitutional status==

The constitutional status of the Emperor has changed dramatically across Okaiken's history:

*'''Absolute monarchy (695 BC – 794 AD)''': The Emperor held supreme religious, military, and political authority.
*'''Regency period (794 – 1185)''': The Emperor's power was gradually eroded by court regents (Sugiwara clan) and cloistered emperors (''Insei'').
*'''Shogunal subordination (1185 – 1870)''': The Emperor was a figurehead under the Hiraizumi, Minato, and Honda Shogunates. During the Honda Dynasty (1610–1870), the "Dual Sovereignty" system formally separated the Imperial and Shogunal lines.
*'''Imperial state (1870 – 1970)''': The [[Saito Restoration]] restored the Emperor to direct political power, though reserve powers were later exploited by militarist factions.
*'''Symbolic role (1970 – 2011)''': The 1970 Constitution reduced the Emperor to a "symbol of the State" with no political authority during the Federal Republic era. Emperor Go-Shōkō was the first emperor to reign under this reduced status, and Emperor Masahito acceded to the throne in 1985 during this period, reigning as a symbolic figurehead for the first 26 years of his reign (the "Silent Years").
*'''Head of State (2011 – present)''': The [[Jutsu Restoration|2011 Sovereignty Act]] amended Article 1 to recognize the Emperor as {{nihongo|Head of State|元首|Genshu}} with reserve powers to resolve parliamentary deadlocks. The [[Kurosawa Doctrine|2014 Defense Normalization Amendment]] further expanded the Emperor's constitutional role by requiring formal assent for military deployments under collective self-defense.

